#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

== Maximal and Hereditary Orders <sec:maximal-hereditary-orders>

Throughout this section, $R$ is a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, and
$A$ is a f.d. $K$-algebra. A _maximal R-order_ in $A$ is an $R$-order in $A$
which is not properly contained in any larger $R$-order in $A$.#idx(
  "maximal order",
)#idx("order", "maximal") For example, when $K$ is an algebraic number field,
the ring $"alg. int." {K}$ is a maximal $ZZ$-order in $K$. As is evident from
Dedekind's theory of ideals, rings of algebraic integers enjoy special
properties which make them easier to handle than their subrings. These
properties also account for the widespread usefulness of such rings in many
branches of algebra and number theory.

Recall that a ring is (left) hereditary if each of its (left) ideals is a
projective module. One of the most useful facts is that every Dedekind domain
$R$ is hereditary. (Indeed, this property characterizes Dedekind domains; see
@prop:dedekind-domain-criteria). More generally, it will turn out that every
maximal order is hereditary, although the converse is false for the
noncommutative case.

In §@ss:maximal-orders-existence we derive some elementary facts about maximal
orders, including the basic result that when $A$ is a separable $K$-algebra,
then every order can be embedded in a maximal order. In
§@ss:maximal-orders-hereditary we give a self-contained (but complicated) proof
that maximal orders are hereditary. The proof includes as a special case the
result that hereditary domains possess a classical ideal theory. Finally,
§@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure lists (without proofs) the basic
structure theorems for maximal and hereditary orders. Much of the global theory
of a Dedekind domain can be obtained from a study of its localizations and
completions. In an analogous manner, many of the properties of maximal and
hereditary orders can be deduced from the knowledge of their completions. This
information can be given in a remarkably explicit form, as will be evident from
the structure theorems given in §@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure.

#heading(level: 3)[
  Existence of Maximal Orders in Separable Algebras
] <ss:maximal-orders-existence>

As in @def:order, an $R$-order in $A$ is a subring $Lambda$ of $A$ such that
$
  R subset.eq Lambda, quad Lambda "is f.g./" R "as module", quad K dot Lambda=A.
$
The embedding of $R$ into the center of $Lambda$ allows us to view all
$Lambda$-modules (and $Lambda$ itself) as $R$-modules, and we do so with no
further comment hereafter.

In this subsection, we shall need some facts about reduced norms and reduced
traces; these are described in the early part of §@ss:reduced-norms. We shall
also
#source(582)
make use of order ideals of $R$-modules, and the relation between discriminants
and order ideals (see §§@ss:modules-dedekind-domains, @ss:duals-lattices).

Our first result shows that an $R$-order may be considered to be a
“noncommutative ring of integers”.

#proposition[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in the $K$-algebra $A$. Then every element
  $x in Lambda$ is integral over $R$. Furthermore,
  $
    "min. pol."_K lr((x)) in R[X], quad
    "char. pol."_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R[X],
  $
  where $X$ is an indeterminate. Therefore
  $ N_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R, quad T_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R, $
  where $N$ and $T$ are the usual norm and trace maps from $A$ to $K$.
] <prop:order-elements-integral>
#proof[
  The Dedekind domain $R$ is integrally closed, and $Lambda$ is f.g./$R$, so the
  result follows from @prop:minimal-polynomial-integrality and
  @prop:characteristic-polynomial-integrality.
]

#corollary[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in the separable $K$-algebra $A$. Then
  $ "red. char. pol."_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R[X] quad "for all" x in Lambda. $
  Therefore
  $ "nr"_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R, quad "tr"_(A slash K) lr((x)) in R, $
  where $"nr"$ and $"tr"$ are the reduced norm and reduced trace maps from $A$
  to $K$.
] <cor:order-reduced-characteristic-integral>
#proof[
  By §@ss:reduced-norms, $"red. char. pol."(x)$ and $"char. pol."(x)$ have the
  same irreducible factors, apart from multiplicities. Since
  $"char. pol."(x) in R[X]$ by @prop:order-elements-integral, each irreducible
  factor of $"char. pol."(x)$ also lies in $R[X]$ by Gauss's Lemma
  @lem:gauss-integrally-closed. Thus $"red. char. pol."(x) in R[X]$, whence
  $"nr"(x),"tr"(x) in R$.
]

Suppose now that $Lambda$ is an $R$-order in a separable $K$-algebra $A$, and
let $m=dim_K A$. By §@ss:reduced-norms, there is a nondegenerate symmetric
$K$-bilinear reduced trace form
$
  "tr":A times A->K, quad "given by" (a,b) mapsto "tr"_(A slash K) lr((a b))
  "for all" a,b in A.
$
We now define the _discriminant_ $d(Lambda)$ of the order $Lambda$ to be the
ideal of $R$ generated by all elements#idx("discriminant ideal")
$ det["tr"(x_i x_j)]_(1<=i,j<=m) quad "with" x_1,dots,x_m in Lambda. $
These ${x_i}$ need not be chosen to be a $K$-basis for $A$; however, whenever
the
#source(583)
${x_i}$ are linearly dependent over $K$, so are the rows of the $m times m$
matrix $["tr"(x_i x_j)]_(1<=i,j<=m)$, and then the determinant equals~0. On the
other hand, this determinant is nonzero when the ${x_i}$ are linearly
independent over $K$, since the trace form is nondegenerate. Further, each entry
$"tr"(x_i x_j) in R$ by @cor:order-reduced-characteristic-integral, and
therefore the discriminant $d(Lambda)$ is a nonzero ideal of $R$. Finally, if it
happens that $Lambda$ has a free $R$-basis ${x_1,dots,x_m}$, then it is easily
seen that $d(Lambda)$ is the principal ideal generated by $det["tr"(x_i x_j)]$.

Let us collect a number of remarks about discriminants:

#proposition[
  Let $A$ be a separable $K$-algebra of dimension $m$. Let $P$ range over the
  maximal ideals of $R$, and let the subscript $P$ denote localization at $P$.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [If $Lambda$ is an $R$-order in $A$, then $Lambda_P$ is an $R_P$-order in
      $A$. Furthermore,
      $
        Lambda=op("⋂", limits: #true)_P Lambda_P quad
        lr(("intersection within" A)).
      $],
    [For each $P$, ${d(Lambda)}_P=d(Lambda_P)$.],
    [Let $Lambda subset.eq Lambda'$, where $Lambda$ and $Lambda'$ are $R$-orders
      in $A$, and let $"ord"(Lambda' slash Lambda)$ be the order ideal of the
      $R$-torsion $R$-module $Lambda' slash Lambda$. Then
      $ d(Lambda)={"ord"(Lambda' slash Lambda)}^2 d(Lambda'). $
      <eq:order-discriminant-index>
      Therefore $d(Lambda) subset.eq d(Lambda')$, with equality if and only if
      $Lambda=Lambda'$.],
  )
] <prop:order-discriminant-localization>
#proof[
  Since $Lambda_P=R_P times.o_R Lambda$, the discussion following
  @cor:localization-chain-conditions shows that, for each $P$, $Lambda_P$ is an
  $R_P$-order in $A$. The second assertion in part~(i) follows from
  @prop:localization-completion-lattices(vi). We leave~(ii) as exercise for the
  reader. Formula @eq:order-discriminant-index has already been established in
  @prop:lattice-discriminant-index, and clearly implies the remaining part of
  (iii).
]

The preceding proposition, though elementary in nature, has the following
important consequence:

#theorem[
  Any $R$-order $Lambda$ in a separable $K$-algebra $A$ is contained in at least
  one maximal $R$-order in $A$.
] <th:order-contained-in-maximal-order>
#proof[
  Consider any strictly increasing chain of $R$-orders in $A$ which begins with
  $Lambda$, say
  $ Lambda subset Lambda_1 subset Lambda_2 subset dots. $
  By @prop:order-discriminant-localization, this chain gives rise to a strictly
  increasing chain of ideals of $R$:
  $ d(Lambda) subset d(Lambda_1) subset d(Lambda_2) subset dots. $
  #source(584)
  This latter chain must terminate, since $R$ is noetherian. Hence the chain of
  orders must also terminate, that is, for some $t$ the order $Lambda_t$ must be
  a maximal order. This completes the proof.
]

The above proof depends heavily on the existence of a nondegenerate bilinear
form from $A times A$ to $K$. For a separable $K$-algebra $A$, the reduced trace
map furnishes such a bilinear form. However, when $char K=0$ and $A$ is
semisimple, the ordinary trace form $T_(A slash K)$ is also nondegenerate. One
can thus prove the preceding theorem by using the ordinary trace form, in the
special case where $A$ is a semisimple algebra over a field of characteristic~0.
However, even in this case there are advantages to using the reduced trace (see,
for example, the proof of Jacobinski's Theorem in
§@sec:group-rings-maximal-orders).

We shall deduce from @th:order-contained-in-maximal-order the existence of
maximal orders in separable algebras. For this purpose, we need only show that
every f.d. $K$-algebra $A$ contains at least one $R$-order $Lambda$. Now every
f.d. $K$-space contains a full $R$-lattice (by §@ss:modules-dedekind-domains, or
@cor:full-order-lattice-existence). Hence $A$ itself contains a full $R$-lattice
$M$. The discussion following @def:left-right-order shows that $O_l lr((M))$ is
an $R$-order in $A$. If $A$ is separable over $K$, then by
@th:order-contained-in-maximal-order it follows that $O_l lr((M))$ is contained
in a maximal $R$-order in $A$. This establishes:

#corollary[
  Every separable $K$-algebra $A$ contains at least one maximal $R$-order.
] <cor:separable-algebra-maximal-order-existence>

We are going to show that a commutative separable $K$-algebra contains a unique
maximal order. To prove this, however, we need some way of deciding when a ring
of integers is actually an order. The key result in this direction is as
follows:

#proposition[
  Let $A$ be a separable $K$-algebra, and let $Lambda$ be a subring of $A$
  containing $R$, such that $K dot Lambda=A$. If every element of $Lambda$ is
  integral over $R$, then $Lambda$ is an $R$-order in $A$.
] <prop:integral-spanning-subring-order>
#proof[
  We need only show that $Lambda$ is f.g. as $R$-module. Since $K dot Lambda=A$,
  we may find elements $x_1,dots,x_m in Lambda$ such that
  $A=op("⨁", limits: #true)_(i=1)^m K x_i$. Let
  $ alpha=det["tr"(x_i x_j)]_(1<=i,j<=m), $
  where $"tr"$ is the reduced trace form from $A$ to $K$. Then $alpha!=0$ since
  $"tr"$ is nondegenerate, while $alpha in R$ since each product $x_i x_j$ lies
  in $Lambda$ and hence is integral over $R$.

  We claim that
  $ Lambda subset.eq alpha^(-1) dot op("⨁", limits: #true)_(i=1)^m R x_i. $
  <eq:integral-subring-trace-bound>
  #source(585)
  Once this is proved, it is clear that $Lambda$ is necessarily f.g./$R$, and
  the proposition is established. To prove the claim, let $y in Lambda$; we may
  write $y=sum_1^m r_i x_i$ with each $r_i in K$. Then
  $ "tr"(y x_j)=sum_(i=1)^m r_i "tr"(x_i x_j), quad 1<=j<=m. $
  Further, $y x_j in Lambda$ for each $j$, whence $"tr"(y x_j) in R$ by
  @cor:order-reduced-characteristic-integral. Solving the above equations for
  the ${r_i}$ by Cramer's Rule, we obtain
  $ r_i=("element of" R) slash alpha, quad 1<=i<=m. $
  Therefore $alpha y=sum alpha r_i x_i in op("⨁", limits: #true) R x_i$. This
  establishes @eq:integral-subring-trace-bound, and finishes the proof.
]

#corollary[
  Let $A$ be a separable $K$-algebra, and let $Gamma$ be a subring of $A$
  containing $R$. If $Gamma$ is f.g./$R$, then $Gamma$ lies in some maximal
  $R$-order in $A$.
] <cor:finite-subring-contained-in-maximal-order>
#proof[
  Let $M$ be any full $R$-lattice in $A$, and set $L=Gamma dot M$. Then $L$ is
  also a full $R$-lattice in $A$; we set $Lambda=O_l lr((L))$, the left order of
  $L$ (see @def:left-right-order). Then $Lambda$ is an $R$-order in $A$, hence
  is contained in a maximal $R$-order in $A$, by
  @th:order-contained-in-maximal-order. But $Gamma subset.eq Lambda$, so $Gamma$
  also lies in some maximal order.
]

We are now ready to prove:

#proposition[
  Let $A$ be a commutative separable $K$-algebra. Then there is a unique maximal
  $R$-order in $A$, namely the integral closure $Lambda$ of $R$ in $A$.
] <prop:commutative-separable-unique-maximal-order>
#proof[
  By definition, $Lambda$ consists of all elements $a in A$ which are integral
  over $R$. Clearly $R subset.eq Lambda$, and (by
  @cor:commuting-integral-elements) $Lambda$ is closed under addition and
  multiplication, since $A$ is commutative. To check that $A=K dot Lambda$, let
  $b in A$. Then $b$ satisfies a polynomial equation
  $
    alpha_0 b^n+alpha_1 b^(n-1)+dots+alpha_n=0, quad alpha_i in R, quad
    alpha_0!=0,
  $
  for some $n$. Multiplying by $alpha_0^(n-1)$, we find at once that $alpha_0 b$
  is integral over $R$, so $alpha_0 b in Lambda$. This shows that
  $A=K dot Lambda$, so $Lambda$ is an $R$-order in $A$, by
  @prop:integral-spanning-subring-order. Finally, $Lambda$ contains every
  $R$-order in $A$, by virtue of @prop:order-elements-integral. Thus, $Lambda$
  is the unique maximal $R$-order in $A$, as asserted.
]

Let us consider briefly the situation where $A$ is not a separable $K$-algebra.
In this case, maximal orders may or may not exist, as is shown by the following
two examples.

#examples[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let $k$ be a field, $char k=p!=0$. Let $K=k(x)$ be a transcendental
      extension of $k$, and let $R=k[x]$, a P.I.D. with field of
      #source(586)
      quotients $K$. Let
      $ A=K(y), quad Lambda=R[y], quad "where" y^p=x. $
      Then $Lambda=k[x,y]=k[y]$, $A=k(x,y)=k(y)$, so $Lambda$ is also a P.I.D.,
      with field of quotients $A$. Thus $Lambda$ is integrally closed in $A$.
      Every $z in A$ which is integral over $R$ is also integral over $Lambda$,
      hence lies in $Lambda$. This shows that $Lambda$ is the integral closure
      of $R$ in $A$, and is the unique maximal $R$-order in $A$. Note that this
      occurs in a situation where $A$ is inseparable over $K$.],
    [Suppose that $R!=K$, and let $A$ be a f.d. $K$-algebra with nonzero
      radical. Given any $R$-order $Lambda$ in $A$, we showed in the proof of
      @prop:nonsemisimple-infinite-lattice-chain that there exists an infinite
      ascending chain @eq:nonsemisimple-order-chain of $R$-orders in $A$,
      starting with $Lambda$. In particular, _every order is properly contained
      in a larger order, so there are no maximal orders in $A$ in this case_.],
  )
] <exm:inseparable-maximal-order-existence>

#examples(name: none, numbered: false)[
  We conclude this subsection by mentioning some examples of maximal orders:

  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [If $K$ is an algebraic number field, then $"alg. int." {K}$ is a maximal
      $ZZ$-order in $K$, by @prop:commutative-separable-unique-maximal-order.],
    [For $G$ a finite group, the integral group ring $R G$ is a maximal order in
      $K G$ if and only if $|G|$ is a unit of $R$ (see
      @prop:group-ring-maximality).],
    [The matrix ring $M_n lr((R))$ is always a maximal order in the matrix
      algebra $M_n lr((K))$, by
      Exercise~@exc:matrix-maximal-order-endomorphisms. Hence for each
      $bold(X) in G L_n lr((K))$, the ring
      $bold(X) dot M_n lr((R)) dot bold(X)^(-1)$ is also a maximal $R$-order in
      $M_n lr((K))$. Thus, when $n>1$, there are many possible maximal orders in
      the matrix algebra $M_n lr((K))$.],
    [Let $L$ be a finite Galois extension of $K$ with Galois group $G$, and let
      $S$ be the integral closure of $R$ in $L$. The _twisted group ring_
      $S compose G$ is defined as the free $S$-module with basis
      ${u_sigma:sigma in G}$, with multiplication defined by the formulas
      $
        u_sigma u_tau=u_(sigma tau), quad u_sigma a=sigma(a)u_sigma, quad
        sigma,tau in G, a in S.
      $
      Then $S compose G$ is a maximal $R$-order in the twisted group algebra
      $L compose G$ if and only if $S$ is unramified over $R$. The result is due
      to Auslander-Goldman~@bib:Auslander1960b; see
      @th:twisted-group-ring-maximality.],
    [Let $L$ be any full $R$-lattice in a f.d. $K$-space $V$. Then $"End"_R L$
      is a maximal $R$-order in $"End"_K V$, by
      §@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure. This result is a generalization
      of~(iii) above.],
  )
] <exm:maximal-order-constructions>

#source(587)
=== Maximal Orders Are Hereditary <ss:maximal-orders-hereditary>

Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in a f.d. $K$-algebra $A$. We call $Lambda$ _left
hereditary_ if every left ideal of $Lambda$ is projective as
$Lambda$-module.#idx("left hereditary")#idx("order", "hereditary") As pointed
out in @prop:dedekind-domain-criteria, an integral domain is hereditary if and
only if it is a Dedekind domain. This result has a beautiful and useful
generalization: every maximal order in a semisimple algebra is both left and
right hereditary. (The converse is false, however! There exist hereditary orders
which are not maximal; see §@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure.)

One way of getting information about an $R$-order $Lambda$ and its
representation theory, is to embed $Lambda$ in a maximal $R$-order $Lambda'$,
and then use properties of $Lambda'$ (see §@sec:finite-representation-type, for
example). In this approach, the fact that $Lambda'$ is hereditary is often of
great importance.

The result that maximal orders are hereditary is an almost trivial corollary of
the structure theorems listed in §@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure; see
@bib:Reiner1975[MO, Theorem 21.4]. This subsection may therefore be skipped in a
first reading, if desired. However, it seems worthwhile to include here a
self-contained proof of the result, which avoids the use of the structure
theorems, and instead follows closely one of the standard proofs that every
ideal in a Dedekind domain factors uniquely into a product of prime ideals.
Indeed, this latter fact is merely a special case of the theorem proved below.
In any case, it is fascinating to see how the usual proof for the commutative
case can be modified to deal with noncommutative arithmetic. Our proof depends
upon elementary manipulations with ideals in maximal orders, and at the end,
benefits from some simplifications given in Swan-Evans~@bib:Swan1970.

The main result is as follows:

#theorem[
  Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in a f.d. semisimple $K$-algebra $A$.
  Then:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The ring $Lambda$ is left and right hereditary, that is, every one-sided
      ideal of $Lambda$ is projective as $Lambda$-module.],
    [Every left (or right) $Lambda$-lattice is $Lambda$-projective.],
    [A left $Lambda$-lattice $M$ is indecomposable if and only if $K M$ is a
      simple left $A$-module.],
  )
] <th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary>

The difficulty lies in proving $Lambda$ left hereditary; the other assertions
are then easily established. Let us show first that the problem can be reduced
to the study of full left $Lambda$-lattices in $A$. Let $M$ be any left ideal of
$Lambda$. Then $K M$ is a left ideal of the semisimple ring $A$, so
$A=K M plus.o W$ for some left ideal $W$ in $A$. We now set $L=Lambda inter W$,
so by @prop:pure-order-sublattice-correspondence $L$ is a left ideal of $Lambda$
such that
$ K(M plus.o L)=K M plus.o K L=K M plus.o W=A. $
#source(588)
If we can prove that the left ideal $M plus.o L$ of $Lambda$ is
$Lambda$-projective, then $M$ is also projective by
§@ss:projective-injective-flat-modules. Now $M plus.o L$ is a full left
$Lambda$-lattice in $A$, that is, $K(M plus.o L)=A$. Thus, in order to prove
that $Lambda$ is left hereditary, it suffices to show that every full left
$Lambda$-lattice in $A$ is $Lambda$-projective.

_For the remainder of this subsection, the word “ideal” will mean a one-sided
(or two-sided) ideal $M$ of $Lambda$ such that $K M=A$._ Keeping this in mind
from now on, we make the following definition:

#definition[
  A _prime ideal_ of the $R$-order $Lambda$ is a proper two-sided ideal
  $frak(p)$ of $Lambda$, such that for each pair of two-sided ideals $I$ and
  $J$,
  $ I dot J subset.eq frak(p) => I subset.eq frak(p) "or" J subset.eq frak(p). $
] <def:prime-order-ideal>
#idx("prime ideal", target: [@def:prime-order-ideal])
#idx("ideal", "prime", target: [@def:prime-order-ideal])

Evidently, the maximal two-sided ideals of $Lambda$ are prime ideals. It can be
shown in this case (where $Lambda$ is an order) that the converse is also true
(see Exercise~@exc:prime-maximal-order-ideals).

In order to prove Theorem~@th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary, we shall make use
of the following theorem#footnote[
  Compare with Exercise~@exc:hereditary-order-completed-radical-ideal. Another
  proof of Theorem~@th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization, based on the structure
  theorems in §@ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure, is given in
  @bib:Reiner1975[MO, Theorem 22.10].
], which is of interest in itself:

#theorem[
  Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in a f.d. semisimple $K$-algebra $A$. Then
  the set of full two-sided $Lambda$-sublattices of $A$ is a free abelian group
  with the prime ideals of $Lambda$ as generators.
] <th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization>
#proof[
  _Step 1._ We show first that every two-sided ideal of $Lambda$ must contain a
  finite product of prime ideals (where the “empty” product is interpreted as
  $Lambda$ itself). If the result is false, there is a maximal counterexample
  $J_0$, since $Lambda$ is noetherian by @cor:noetherian-finite-algebra-modules.
  Since $J_0$ is not a prime ideal (obviously), there exist two-sided ideals $I$
  and $J$, neither contained in $J_0$, such that $I J subset.eq J_0$. But then
  $I+J_0$ and $J+J_0$ both properly contain $J_0$, hence are not
  counterexamples, and thus each contains a product of prime ideals. But then so
  does $J_0$, since $J_0 supset.eq (I+J_0)(J+J_0)$. This contradiction shows
  that every two-sided ideal contains a product of prime ideals.

  We now define “inverses” of ideals as follows: given any full $R$-lattice $L$
  in $A$, let
  $
    L^(-1) & ={x in A:L dot x dot L subset.eq L} \
           & ={x in A:L x subset.eq O_l lr((L))}
             ={x in A:x L subset.eq O_r lr((L))},
  $ <eq:inverse-order-lattice>
  #idx("inverse ideal", target: [@eq:inverse-order-lattice])
  #idx("ideal", "inverse", target: [@eq:inverse-order-lattice])
  where the left order $O_l lr((L))$ of $L$, and the right order $O_r lr((L))$,
  are defined as in
  #source(589)
  @def:left-right-order. There are obvious inclusions
  $
    O_l lr((L^(-1))) supset.eq O_r lr((L)), quad
    O_r lr((L^(-1))) supset.eq O_l lr((L)).
  $ <eq:inverse-lattice-order-inclusions>
  Furthermore, $L supset.eq M$ implies that $L^(-1) subset.eq M^(-1)$, for any
  pair of full $R$-lattices $L$ and $M$ in $A$ which have the same left order.
  We do not know at this point that $(L^(-1))^(-1)=L$, though indeed this
  formula turns out to be true when $L$ is a $Lambda$-lattice for a maximal
  order $Lambda$ (see Exercise~@exc:maximal-lattice-inverse-right-order).

  _Step 2._ Let $J$ be a proper two-sided ideal of the maximal order $Lambda$.
  It is clear that $J^(-1) supset.eq Lambda$. We now show that
  $J^(-1) supset Lambda$, by assuming that $J^(-1)=Lambda$ and obtaining a
  contradiction. We may choose a prime ideal $frak(p)$ of $Lambda$ with
  $J subset.eq frak(p) subset Lambda$; then
  $frak(p)^(-1) subset.eq J^(-1)=Lambda$ whence $frak(p)^(-1)=Lambda$. To show
  that this is impossible, note first that $K frak(p)=A$, so $1_A=lambda x$ for
  some $lambda in K$ and $x in frak(p)$. Clearing the denominator, we obtain a
  nonzero element $alpha in R inter frak(p)$. By Step~1, $alpha Lambda$ contains
  a product of prime ideals, and so we may write
  $ frak(p) supset.eq alpha Lambda supset.eq frak(p)_1 dots frak(p)_r, $
  with the ${frak(p)_i}$ prime ideals, and where $r$ is minimal for this
  $alpha$. Since $frak(p) supset.eq frak(p)_1 dots frak(p)_r$, it follows that
  $frak(p) supset.eq frak(p)_j$ for some $j$, whence $frak(p)=frak(p)_j$. Thus
  we may rewrite the above display as
  $ frak(p) supset.eq alpha Lambda supset.eq B dot frak(p) dot C $
  where $B C$ is a product of $r-1$ prime ideals of $Lambda$. Using the fact
  that $Lambda$ is a maximal order, we then obtain:
  $
    alpha^(-1)B frak(p)C subset.eq Lambda =>alpha^(-1)B frak(p)C dot B
    subset.eq B
    =>alpha^(-1)frak(p)C B subset.eq O_r lr((B))=Lambda
    \ =>alpha^(-1)C B subset.eq frak(p)^(-1)=Lambda
    =>alpha Lambda supset.eq C B,
  $
  contradicting the minimality of $r$. This completes the proof that
  $J^(-1) supset Lambda$.

  _Step 3._ Let $L$ be a full $R$-lattice in $A$ such that $O_l lr((L))$ is
  maximal; we claim that
  $ L dot L^(-1)=O_l lr((L)). $ <eq:maximal-left-order-inverse-product>
  Indeed, let $Gamma=O_l lr((L))$, $J=L dot L^(-1)$, so $J$ is a two-sided ideal
  in the maximal order $Gamma$. Then
  $
    J J^(-1) subset.eq Gamma=>L L^(-1) dot J^(-1) subset.eq Gamma
    =>L^(-1)J^(-1) subset.eq L^(-1)
    =>J^(-1) subset.eq O_r lr((L^(-1))).
  $
  But $O_r lr((L^(-1))) supset.eq Gamma$ by
  @eq:inverse-lattice-order-inclusions, so $O_r lr((L^(-1)))=Gamma$ (since
  $Gamma$ is maximal), and thus $J^(-1) subset.eq Gamma$. Hence $J=Gamma$ by
  Step~2, and @eq:maximal-left-order-inverse-product is proved. In the same
  manner it
  #source(590)
  follows that if $N$ is a full $R$-lattice in $A$ whose right order
  $O_r lr((N))$ is maximal, then $N^(-1)N=O_r lr((N))$.

  Consequently, for every two-sided ideal $J$ of the maximal order $Lambda$, we
  have
  $ J J^(-1)=J^(-1)J=Lambda. $ <eq:maximal-order-ideal-inverse>
  Further, we may choose a nonzero $beta in R$ such that
  $beta J^(-1) subset.eq Lambda$; then by the above,
  $
    (beta J^(-1))(beta J^(-1))^(-1)=Lambda,
    quad "that is," (J^(-1))(J^(-1))^(-1)=Lambda.
  $
  This proves that
  $ (J^(-1))^(-1)=J. $
  Let us deduce from this that every two-sided ideal $J$ of $Lambda$ is uniquely
  expressible as a finite product of prime ideals, and that multiplication of
  such ideals is commutative. (If $J=Lambda$, write $J$ as an “empty” product of
  prime ideals!) Suppose the result is false, and let $J$ be a maximal
  counterexample. Then $J$ is not prime, so there exists a prime ideal $frak(p)$
  with $J subset frak(p) subset Lambda$, whence
  $ J subset.eq J frak(p)^(-1) subset.eq Lambda. $
  If $J=J frak(p)^(-1)$ then $frak(p)^(-1) subset.eq O_r lr((J))=Lambda$, which
  is impossible by Step~2. Hence $J frak(p)^(-1) supset J$, and so
  $J frak(p)^(-1)$ is a product $frak(p)_1 dots frak(p)_k$ of primes; but then
  $J=J frak(p)^(-1) dot frak(p)=frak(p)_1 dots frak(p)_k frak(p)$, a
  contradiction. This shows that every two-sided ideal of $Lambda$ is
  expressible as a product of prime ideals.

  Now let $frak(p),frak(q)$ be distinct primes; then
  $
    frak(p)^(-1)frak(q)frak(p) subset.eq frak(p)^(-1) dot Lambda dot frak(p)
    subset.eq Lambda,
  $
  and
  $ frak(p)(frak(p)^(-1)frak(q)frak(p))=frak(q)frak(p) subset.eq frak(q). $
  Thus $frak(q)$ contains the product of $frak(p)$ and
  $frak(p)^(-1)frak(q)frak(p)$, a pair of two-sided ideals of $Lambda$. Since
  $frak(q)$ is prime, this gives $frak(q) supset.eq frak(p)^(-1)frak(q)frak(p)$,
  so $frak(p)frak(q) supset.eq frak(q)frak(p)$. The reverse inclusion holds by
  symmetry, and thus $frak(p)frak(q)=frak(q)frak(p)$.

  Now suppose that the ${frak(p)_i}$, ${frak(q)_j}$ are prime ideals for which
  $ frak(p)_1 dots frak(p)_r=frak(q)_1 dots frak(q)_s. $
  Then $frak(p)_1 supset.eq product frak(q)_j$, so $frak(p)_1=frak(q)_l$ for
  some $l$. Multiply by $frak(p)_1^(-1)$ and repeat the argument, so as to
  deduce eventually that the ${frak(p)_i}$ are just a rearrangement of the
  ${frak(q)_j}$. This shows that every two-sided ideal of $Lambda$ is uniquely
  expressible as a finite product of prime ideals (apart from order of
  occurrence), and that multiplication of such ideals is commutative.

  #source(591)
  To complete the proof of @th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization, it suffices to
  show that every full two-sided $Lambda$-lattice $J$ in $A$ can be expressed as
  a product of prime ideals and inverses of prime ideals. But
  $alpha J subset.eq Lambda$ for some nonzero $alpha in R$, and
  $J=(alpha Lambda)^(-1) dot alpha J$. If we write
  $alpha Lambda=product frak(p)_i$ and $alpha J=product frak(q)_j$, then we
  obtain $J={product frak(p)_i^(-1)}{product frak(q)_j}$, as desired. This
  establishes @th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization.
]

(We should point out an important special case of
@th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization. Let $E$ be an algebraic number field, and
let $S="alg. int." {E}$. Then $S$ is the integral closure of $ZZ$ in $E$, so $S$
is a maximal $ZZ$-order in $E$ by
@prop:commutative-separable-unique-maximal-order. It then follows from
@th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization that every nonzero ideal of $S$ is
uniquely expressible as a product of maximal ideals of $S$, and that the group
of fractional $S$-ideals of $E$ is the free abelian group generated by these
maximal ideals.)

Let us continue with our main objective: the proof of
Theorem~@th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary. We show next that every two-sided
ideal $J$ of $Lambda$ is both left and right $Lambda$-projective. Since
$J^(-1)J=Lambda$ by @eq:maximal-order-ideal-inverse, we may write
$ 1=sum_(i=1)^n x_i m_i, quad x_i in J^(-1), quad m_i in J, $
for some choices of elements ${x_i}$, ${m_i}$. Now define
$
  phi:Lambda^((n))->J quad "by" phi(a_1, dots, a_n)=sum a_i m_i,
  quad a_i in Lambda,
$
$
  psi:J->Lambda^((n)) quad "by" psi(m)=(m x_1,dots,m x_n), quad m in J.
$
Then $phi$ and $psi$ are left $Lambda$-homomorphisms such that $phi psi$ is the
identity map on $J$. Therefore $J$ is a direct summand of $Lambda^((n))$ (as
left $Lambda$-module), and hence $J$ is left $Lambda$-projective. Likewise, $J$
is projective as right $Lambda$-module. (Compare this argument with the proof of
the Dual Basis Lemma~@lem:dual-basis.)

To complete the proof of @th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary, we shall use some
elementary facts about the _homological dimension_ $"hd"(T)$ of a left
$Lambda$-module $T$.#idx("homological dimension") (This approach saves two pages
of manipulation!) By definition, $"hd"(T)$ is the smallest integer $k$ (if such
exists) such that
$ "Ext"_Lambda^n lr((T,ast))=0 quad "for all" n>k. $
(Write $"hd"(T)=infinity$ if no such $k$ exists.) We now prove that
$ "hd"(T)<=1 quad "for every f.g." R"-torsion" Lambda"-module" T. $
<eq:torsion-order-module-homological-dimension>
(In the above, $T$ is a f.g. left $Lambda$-module which is a torsion
$R$-module.) Taking this for granted for the moment, let $M$ be any full left
ideal of $Lambda$. Then there is an exact sequence of left $Lambda$-modules:
$ 0->M->Lambda->T->0. $

#source(592)
Since $K dot M=K dot Lambda$, it follows that $T$ is a f.g. $R$-torsion
$Lambda$-module, whence $"Ext"_Lambda^2 lr((T,ast))=0$. But
$
  "Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((Lambda,ast))->"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((M,ast))->"Ext"_Lambda^2
  lr((T,ast))
$
is exact by @th:ext-long-exact-sequence, and $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((Lambda,ast))=0$
by @rem:ext-properties(v). Hence we obtain $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((M,ast))=0$, so
$M$ is $Lambda$-projective by @rem:ext-properties(v). Thus, every full left
ideal of $Lambda$ is projective. It follows from Step~1 that $Lambda$ is left
hereditary, as claimed.

We shall now prove @eq:torsion-order-module-homological-dimension. We have
$beta T=0$ for some nonzero $beta in R$, so $T$ may be viewed as a f.g. module
over the ring $R slash beta R$. But $R slash beta R$ is artinian and noetherian,
whence so is the $(R slash beta R)$-module $T$, by §@ss:finiteness-conditions.
Since every $Lambda$-submodule of $T$ is automatically an $R$-submodule of $T$,
it follows that $T$ is both artinian and noetherian as left $Lambda$-module.
Therefore $T$ has a composition series @prop:composition-series-finiteness. We
shall prove @eq:torsion-order-module-homological-dimension by induction on the
$Lambda$-composition length $l(T)$ of the $Lambda$-module $T$.

Consider first the case where $l(T)=1$, so $T$ is a simple left $Lambda$-module,
and $beta T=0$ for some nonzero $beta in R$. By
@th:maximal-order-ideal-factorization, we may write
$ beta Lambda=product_(i=1)^r frak(p)_i^(e_i), $
with the ${frak(p)_i}$ distinct prime ideals of $Lambda$. For each $i$,
$frak(p)_i T$ is a $Lambda$-submodule of the simple module $T$, and thus
$frak(p)_i T$ is either~0 or $T$. If $frak(p)_i T=T$ for each $i$, then
$beta T=T$, which is impossible. Hence there exists an index $i$ such that
$frak(p)_i T=0$. Then $T$ is also a simple left $overline(Lambda)$-module, where
$overline(Lambda)=Lambda slash frak(p)_i$. Now $overline(Lambda)$ is a simple
artinian ring by Exercise~@exc:prime-maximal-order-ideals, so $T$ is isomorphic
to a direct summand of $overline(Lambda)$ (as left $overline(Lambda)$-module).
But then $T$ is also isomorphic to a direct summand of $overline(Lambda)$ as
left $Lambda$-module, whence (by @rem:ext-properties(iv)) we have
$"hd"_Lambda lr((T))<="hd"_Lambda lr((overline(Lambda)))$. But
$ 0->frak(p)_i->Lambda->overline(Lambda)->0 $
is an exact sequence of left $Lambda$-modules, and $frak(p)_i$ is left
$Lambda$-projective by the earlier discussion. Hence
$"hd"_Lambda lr((overline(Lambda)))<=1$ by @th:ext-long-exact-sequence, so also
$"hd"_Lambda lr((T))<=1$, which proves
@eq:torsion-order-module-homological-dimension whenever $T$ is a simple
$Lambda$-module.

Suppose now that $l(T)=d>1$, and that $"hd"(T')<=1$ for all f.g. $R$-torsion
left $Lambda$-modules $T'$ with $l(T')=d-1$. Let $T_0$ be any simple
$Lambda$-submodule of $T$. Then there is an exact sequence
$ 0->T_0->T->T'->0, $
where $l(T')=d-1$. Then $"hd"(T_0)<=1$, $"hd"(T')<=1$, whence also $"hd"(T)<=1$
by @th:ext-long-exact-sequence. This completes the proof of
@eq:torsion-order-module-homological-dimension.

We have now completed the proof that $Lambda$ is left hereditary. To prove that
$Lambda$ is also right hereditary, we may use either of two arguments. On the
one hand, the desired result follows from a theorem of M.~Auslander: _If the
ring $Lambda$_#idx("Auslander's Theorem")
#source(593)
_is left and right noetherian, then $Lambda$ is left hereditary if and only if
$Lambda$ is right hereditary_.#idx("Auslander's Theorem") (For a proof, see
Rotman~@bib:Rotman1979, Cor. 9.23.)

A second approach is as follows: let $Lambda^circle.small$ denote the opposite
ring of $Lambda$. Then $Lambda^circle.small$ is a maximal $R$-order in the
semisimple $K$-algebra $A^circle.small$, so $Lambda^circle.small$ is left
hereditary by the previous steps. Every right $Lambda$-module may be viewed as
left $Lambda^circle.small$-module, and vice versa. Hence $Lambda$ is right
hereditary, as claimed.

Assertion~(ii) of Theorem~@th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary follows at once from
Exercise~@exc:lattice-embedding-finite-free-order-module and Proposition
@prop:hereditary-free-submodules. Finally, let $M$ be any left $Lambda$-lattice.
If $M$ is decomposable, then so is $K M$, whence $K M$ is not a simple
$A$-module. Conversely, suppose that $W$ is a nonzero proper submodule of $K M$,
and set $L=W inter M$. By @cor:full-order-lattice-existence there is then an
exact sequence of $Lambda$-lattices:
$ 0->L->M->M slash L->0. $
But $M slash L$ is $Lambda$-projective by @th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary(ii),
whence the sequence splits: $M tilde.equiv L plus.o (M slash L)$ as
$Lambda$-modules. We have thus shown that $M$ is decomposable if and only if
$K M$ is not simple, which completes the proof of assertion~(iii), and
establishes the theorem.

#remark(numbered: false)[
  There exist left hereditary rings which are not right hereditary; for example
  (see L.~Small~@bib:Small1966), the ring
  $ {mat(a, 0; b, c):a in ZZ,b,c in QQ} $
  is left but not right hereditary.
]

#heading(level: 3)[
  Structure Theorems for Maximal and Hereditary Orders
] <ss:maximal-hereditary-order-structure>

Throughout this subsection, let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with quotient field
$K$. We shall summarize without proof a number of basic facts about maximal and
hereditary orders. Detailed proofs may be found in @bib:Reiner1975[MO], and we
shall not repeat them here. Of course, we already know from
§@ss:maximal-orders-hereditary that every maximal order is hereditary, so we may
expect that the theorems about the structure of maximal orders will generalize
to the case of hereditary orders.

The first part of the discussion shows that for both maximal and hereditary
orders, the problem of determining their structure can be reduced to the case of
orders in simple algebras over local fields. There is a further reduction to the
case of orders in division algebras over local fields, in which case we have
extremely explicit information. We conclude with a discussion of the behavior of
maximal and hereditary orders under ground ring extension.

To begin with, we quote a result showing that the study of maximal and
hereditary orders can be reduced to the case of orders in central simple
algebras.

#theorem[
  Let $A$ be a separable f.d. $K$-algebra, and write
  $ A=A_1 plus.o dots plus.o A_t, quad 1=e_1+dots+e_t, quad e_i in A_i, $
  #source(594)
  where the ${A_i}$ are the Wedderburn components of $A$, and the ${e_i}$ the
  corresponding central primitive idempotents. For each $i$, let $K_i$ be the
  center of $A_i$, and $R_i$ the integral closure of $R$ in $K_i$. Then:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For each maximal $R$-order $Lambda$ in $A$, we have
      $Lambda=op("⨁", limits: #true)_(i=1)^t Lambda e_i$, and for each $i$,
      $Lambda e_i$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A_i$.],
    [Conversely, if $Lambda_i$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A_i$ for each $i$,
      then $op("⨁", limits: #true) Lambda_i$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A$.],
    [An $R$-order $Lambda_i$ in $A_i$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A_i$ if and
      only if $Lambda_i$ is a maximal $R_i$-order in $A_i$.],
    [In the special case where $R$ is a complete d.v.r., the above assertions
      remain true whenever $A$ is a semisimple algebra, whether or not separable
      over $K$.],
  )
] <th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components>

#theorem-suffix(base: [@th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components], suffix: "a")[
  The preceding Theorem~@th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components remains true if
  “maximal” is replaced by “hereditary” throughout.
] <th:hereditary-orders-wedderburn-components>

For the proof of @th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components, see
@bib:Reiner1975[MO (10.5)]; for @th:hereditary-orders-wedderburn-components, see
@bib:Reiner1975[MO (10.8), (10.9) and (40.7)]. The proofs are rather
straightforward.

Our next results show the connection between the global and local cases. Let $P$
range over all maximal ideals of $R$, and let $R_P$ denote localization at $P$,
and $hat(R)_P$ $P$-adic completion. If $Lambda$ is any $R$-order in a f.d.
$K$-algebra $A$, then $Lambda_P$ is an $R_P$-order in $A$, and $hat(Lambda)_P$
is an $hat(R)_P$-order in $hat(A)_P$. We have:

#theorem[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For any $R$-order $Lambda$ in a f.d. $K$-algebra $A$, we have
      $
        Lambda=op("⋂", limits: #true)_P Lambda_P=op("⋂", limits: #true)_P
        (hat(Lambda)_P inter A).
      $],
    [$Lambda$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A$ if and only if, for each $P$,
      $Lambda_P$ is a maximal $R_P$-order in $A$.],
    [Let $P$ be fixed, and let $Delta$ be an $R_P$-order in $A$. Then $Delta$ is
      a maximal $R_P$-order in $A$ if and only if its $P$-adic completion
      $hat(Delta)_P$ is a maximal $hat(R)_P$-order in $hat(A)_P$.],
  )
] <th:maximal-orders-localization>

#theorem-suffix(base: [@th:maximal-orders-localization], suffix: "a")[
  The preceding Theorem~@th:maximal-orders-localization remains true if
  “maximal” is replaced by “hereditary” throughout.
] <th:hereditary-orders-localization>

Assertion~@th:maximal-orders-localization(i) is clear from
@prop:localization-completion-lattices, and is restated here for convenience.
For @th:maximal-orders-localization(ii) see @bib:Reiner1975[MO (11.2)], and
@bib:Reiner1975[MO (11.5)] for @th:maximal-orders-localization(iii). For the
proof of @th:hereditary-orders-localization, see @bib:Reiner1975[MO (3.24),
  (2.22) and (2.39)].

#source(595)
The theorems above show that the study of maximal (and hereditary) orders can be
reduced to the study of such orders in central simple algebras over local
fields. The key to the latter situation is the fact that if $D$ is a division
algebra over a complete local field $K$, and $R$ the d.v.r. in $K$, then $D$ has
a unique maximal $R$-order $Delta$. This order $Delta$ is the integral closure
of $R$ in $D$, and behaves in every way like a d.v.r., except for the fact that
multiplication is not commutative. If $R$ has a finite residue class field
$overline(R)$, as is the case most often encountered, even stronger results are
available. Finally, the passage from $D$ to a matrix algebra over $D$ is
accomplished by use of the Morita Theorems.

The main facts concerning maximal orders in division rings are summarized in the
following theorem; for proofs, see @bib:Reiner1975[MO (12.8), (13.2), (13.3) and
  (14.3)].

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a complete d.v.r. with exponential valuation#footnote[
    See §@ss:valuations-completions.
  ] $v$, prime element $pi$, field of quotients $K$, and residue class field
  $overline(R)=R slash pi R$. Let $D$ be a skewfield with center $K$, and let
  $dim_K D=m^2$, $m$ finite.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Define
      $ w(a)=1/m^2 v(N_(D slash K) a), quad a in D, $
      where $N_(D slash K)$ is the usual norm from $D$ to $K$. Then $w$ is a
      discrete valuation on $D$ which extends $v$. The valuation ring of $w$ is
      given by
      $ Delta={a in D:w(a)>=0}={a in D:N_(D slash K) a in R}. $
      This ring $Delta$ is the integral closure of $R$ in $D$, and is the unique
      maximal $R$-order in $D$. Put $e=[w(D^dot):v(K^dot)]$, the index of the
      value groups.],
    [Let $pi_D$ be a prime element of $Delta$, that is, an element for which
      $w(pi_D)=1/e$. Set $frak(p)=pi_D Delta$,
      $overline(Delta)=Delta slash frak(p)$. Then $frak(p)$ is a two-sided ideal
      of $Delta$, and every nonzero two-sided ideal of $Delta$ is a power of
      $frak(p)$. Further,
      $ frak(p)^k=pi_D^k Delta=Delta pi_D^k={a in Delta:w(a)>=k/e}. $
      Every one-sided ideal of $Delta$ is a two-sided ideal.],
    [The residue class ring $overline(Delta)$ is a skewfield whose center
      contains $overline(R)$, and
      $ f=dim_(overline(R)) overline(Delta)="inertial degree of" D "over" K $
      #idx("inertial degree")
      is finite. We have
      $
        dim_K D=m^2=e f, quad "where" e=1/w(pi_D)="ramification index of"
        D "over" K.
      $
      #idx("ramification index")],
    [#source(596)
      If the field $overline(R)$ is finite, then
      $ e=f=m=sqrt(dim_K D). $],
  )
] <th:division-algebra-maximal-order-valuation>

Using the Morita Theorems to extend the above to the classification of maximal
orders in a full matrix algebra $M_r lr((D))$, we obtain (see @bib:Reiner1975[MO
  (17.3), (17.4)]):

#theorem[
  Keep the above notation, and let $A=M_r lr((D))$.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let $Lambda=M_r lr((Delta))$. Then $Lambda$ is a maximal $R$-order in $A$,
      and its unique maximal two-sided ideal is $pi_D Lambda$. Every nonzero
      two-sided ideal of $Lambda$ is of the form
      $
        (pi_D Lambda)^k=pi_D^k Lambda=M_r lr((pi_D^k Delta)), quad k=0,1,2,dots.
      $
      We have $"rad" Lambda=pi_D Lambda$, and
      $
        Lambda slash "rad" Lambda tilde.equiv M_r lr((overline(Delta))), quad
        "where" overline(Delta)=Delta slash pi_D Delta.
      $],
    [The maximal $R$-orders in $A$ are precisely the rings $u Lambda u^(-1)$,
      with $u$ a unit of $A$.],
    [Let $A="End"_D V$, where $V$ is a right $D$-space of dimension $r$. Let
      $Gamma$ be any maximal $R$-order in $A$; then there exists a full right
      $Delta$-lattice $M$ in $V$, which is free of rank $r$ as $Delta$-module,
      such that $Gamma="End"_Delta M$. Conversely, each such endomorphism ring
      is a maximal order.],
  )
] <th:local-matrix-maximal-orders>

This result readily carries over to the case where the d.v.r. $R$ is not
necessarily complete (see @bib:Reiner1975[MO (18.3), (18.7), and (18.10)]):

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a d.v.r. with maximal ideal $P$, and let $hat(R)$ be its $P$-adic
  completion, and $hat(K)$ the $P$-adic completion of $K$. Let $A$ be a central
  simple $K$-algebra.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The maximal $R$-orders $Lambda$ in $A$ are of the form
      $Lambda=A inter hat(Lambda)$, with $hat(Lambda)$ ranging over the maximal
      $hat(R)$-orders in $hat(A)$. Further, $hat(Lambda)$ is the completion of
      $Lambda$. If $"rad" hat(Lambda)$ denotes the unique maximal two-sided
      ideal of $hat(Lambda)$, then
      $
        "rad" Lambda=Lambda inter "rad"
        hat(Lambda)="unique maximal two-sided ideal of" Lambda.
      $
      Further, $"rad" hat(Lambda)$ is the completion of $"rad" Lambda$, and
      $
        Lambda slash "rad" Lambda tilde.equiv hat(Lambda) slash "rad"
        hat(Lambda).
      $],
    [#source(597)
      Let $hat(A)=M_r lr((D))$, where $D$ is a skewfield. Then $D$ has center
      $hat(K)$, and has a unique maximal $hat(R)$-order $Delta$ (as in
      @th:division-algebra-maximal-order-valuation). We have
      $
        Lambda slash "rad" Lambda tilde.equiv hat(Lambda) slash "rad"
        hat(Lambda) tilde.equiv M_r lr((overline(Delta))),
      $
      where $overline(Delta)=Delta slash pi_D Delta$ is a skewfield of finite
      dimension $f$ over $overline(R)$. Further,
      $ P Lambda=("rad" Lambda)^e, quad "where" e f=dim_(hat(K)) D. $
      If $overline(R)$ is finite, then
      $ e=f=sqrt(dim_(hat(K)) D). $],
    [Every one-sided ideal of $Lambda$ is a principal ideal. More
      generally#footnote[
        The result for lattices implies the assertion about ideals. Indeed, if
        $M$ is a left ideal of $Lambda$, then $K M$ is a left ideal of $A$.
        Therefore $K M=A x=K dot Lambda x$ for some $x in A$, and so
        $M tilde.equiv Lambda x$.
      ], let $M$ and $N$ be a pair of left $Lambda$-lattices. Then
      $M tilde.equiv N<=>K M tilde.equiv K N$ as $A$-modules $<=>$ $M$ and $N$
      have the same $R$-rank.],
  )
] <th:dvr-maximal-orders-completion-lattices>

Returning to the global case, we may combine the results of Theorems
@th:maximal-orders-localization and @th:local-matrix-maximal-orders to obtain:

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be any Dedekind domain, with quotient field $K$. Let $D$ be a f.d.
  division algebra over its center $K$. Let $V$ be a f.d. right vector space
  over $D$, and let $A="End"_D V$, a central simple $K$-algebra.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let $Delta$ be some fixed maximal $R$-order in $D$. Then for every full
      right $Delta$-lattice $M$ in $V$, the ring $"End"_Delta M$ is a maximal
      $R$-order in $A$.],
    [Let $Delta$ be as above. Then each maximal $R$-order $Gamma$ in $A$ is of
      the form $Gamma="End"_Delta N$ for some full right $Delta$-lattice $N$ in
      $V$.],
    [The maximal order $Delta$ is not necessarily unique. However, any two
      maximal orders in $D$ must be Morita equivalent. Therefore each pair of
      maximal orders in $A$ must be Morita equivalent.],
  )
] <th:global-maximal-orders-endomorphism-lattices>

(The proofs are given in @bib:Reiner1975[MO (21.6), (21.7)] and
@bib:Reiner1975[MO Exercise 22.11]. Also see
Exercise~@exc:matrix-maximal-order-endomorphisms below.)

It is extremely useful to know whether maximal orders remain maximal after
ground ring extension. As above, the problem reduces to the case of simple
algebras over local fields, and for this case the question is completely settled
by:

#source(598)
#theorem(title: [Janusz~@bib:Janusz1979])[
  Let $R$ be a complete d.v.r. with finite residue field and quotient field
  $K$.#ed-note[
    The finite-residue-field hypothesis restores the local-field setting of
    Janusz~@bib:Janusz1979, Introduction, Theorem. It is needed for the
    necessity assertions here and in @th:janusz-hereditary-order-tensor-product.
    For example, let $R=QQ[[t]]$ and $Delta=bb(H)_QQ[[t]]$, where $bb(H)_QQ$ is
    the rational Hamilton division algebra. Then $Delta$ is a noncommutative
    maximal hereditary order, but
    $Delta times.o_R Delta tilde.equiv M_4 lr((R))$. Indeed, leading
    coefficients show that $bb(H)_QQ lr(((t)))$ is a division algebra and every
    one-sided ideal of $Delta$ is a power of $t Delta$; the tensor isomorphism
    is induced by $(a times.o b)(x)=a x overline(b)$. This counterexample is an
    independent argument.
  ] For $i=1,2$, let $A_i$ be a central simple $E_i$-algebra, where $E_i$ is a
  finite separable extension of $K$, and $S_i$ the integral closure of $R$ in
  $E_i$. Let $Lambda_i$ be a maximal $R$-order in $A_i$, $i=1,2$. Then
  $Lambda_1 times.o_R Lambda_2$ is a maximal $R$-order in the semisimple
  $K$-algebra $A_1 times.o_K A_2$ if and only if one of the orders, say
  $Lambda_1$, satisfies the following two conditions:#footnote[
    See §@ss:dedekind-ideal-theory for the definitions of “unramified” and of
    $f_i$. The index of $A_2$ is defined as in
    @def:local-index-capacity-ramification.
  ]
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$Lambda_1 tilde.equiv M_n lr((S_1))$ and $S_1$ is unramified over $R$,],
    [$"G.C.D."(f_1,f_2 m)="G.C.D."(f_1,f_2)$, where
      $
        m="index of" A_2, quad f_i="residue class degree of" S_i "relative to"
        R quad (i=1,2).
      $],
  )
] <th:janusz-maximal-order-tensor-product>


#idx("Janusz's Theorem", target: [@th:janusz-maximal-order-tensor-product])

We omit the proof, which is based on calculations of discriminants. Janusz also
showed that if $Lambda_1 times.o_R Lambda_2$ is a maximal order, then $Lambda_1$
and $Lambda_2$ must both be maximal. As a special case of the above theorem, we
have:

#corollary[
  Keep the above notation, and let $Lambda_1=S_1$, $A_1=E_1$. Then
  $S_1 times.o_R Lambda_2$ is a maximal $S_1$-order in $E_1 times.o_K A_2$
  whenever $S_1$ is unramified over $R$ and
  $ "G.C.D."(f_1,f_2 m)="G.C.D."(f_1,f_2). $
] <cor:unramified-maximal-order-scalar-extension>

Turning next to hereditary orders, we shall give an analogue of
Theorem~@th:local-matrix-maximal-orders which describes the structure of
hereditary orders in simple algebras over local fields. Some notation will be
helpful. Let ${frak(a)_(i j):1<=i,j<=r}$ be a set of ideals in a ring $Gamma$.
Then the expression
$
  mat(
    delim: "[",
    (frak(a)_11), (frak(a)_12), dots, (frak(a)_(1 r));
    (frak(a)_21), (frak(a)_22), dots, (frak(a)_(2 r));
    dots, dots, dots, dots;
    (frak(a)_(r 1)), (frak(a)_(r 2)), dots, (frak(a)_(r r))
  )^({n_1,dots,n_r})
$
denotes the set of all matrices
$
  mat(
    delim: "[",
    upright(bold(T))_11, upright(bold(T))_12, dots, upright(bold(T))_(1 r);
    upright(bold(T))_21, upright(bold(T))_22, dots, upright(bold(T))_(2 r);
    dots, dots, dots, dots;
    upright(bold(T))_(r 1), upright(bold(T))_(r 2), dots, upright(bold(T))_(r r)
  )
$
where for each pair $(i,j)$, the matrix $upright(bold(T))_(i j)$ ranges over all
$n_i times n_j$ matrices with entries in the ideal $frak(a)_(i j)$.

We are now ready to state the main result, whose proof is given in
@bib:Reiner1975[MO (39.14), (39.23) and (39.24)]:

#source(599)
#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a complete d.v.r. with quotient field $K$, $D$ a f.d. division
  algebra over its center $K$, $Delta$ the integral closure of $R$ in $D$, and
  $frak(p)="rad" Delta$ (as in @th:division-algebra-maximal-order-valuation).
  Let $A=M_n lr((D))$, and let $Lambda$ be a hereditary $R$-order in $A$. Then
  we have:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [After a similarity transformation $Lambda->bold(X) Lambda bold(X)^(-1)$,
      for some $bold(X) in G L_n lr((D))$, we may write
      $
        Lambda=mat(
          delim: "[",
          (Delta), (frak(p)), (frak(p)), dots, (frak(p));
          (Delta), (Delta), (frak(p)), dots, (frak(p));
          (Delta), (Delta), (Delta), dots, (frak(p));
          dots, dots, dots, dots, dots;
          (Delta), (Delta), (Delta), dots, (Delta)
        )^({n_1,dots,n_r})
      $
      for some positive integers ${n_i}$ with sum $n$.],
    [$"rad" Lambda$ is obtained from the above display by replacing each
      $(Delta)$ on the main diagonal by $(frak(p))$. Therefore
      $
        Lambda slash "rad" Lambda tilde.equiv product.co_(i=1)^r M_(n_i)
        lr((overline(Delta))), quad "where" overline(Delta)=Delta slash frak(p).
      $],
    [The isomorphism invariants of the hereditary order $Lambda$ are its _type_
      $r$ and the cycle ${n_1,dots,n_r}$.#idx("type (of hereditary order)")
      These determine $Lambda$ up to similarity.],
    [Let $M$ be the space of $n times 1$ column vectors over $Delta$. Then
      $ {("rad" Lambda)^i M:0<=i<=r-1} $
      is a full set of nonisomorphic indecomposable $Lambda$-lattices. For each
      $i$, $("rad" Lambda)^i M$ consists of all column vectors whose first
      $n_1+dots+n_i$ entries lie in $frak(p)$, and whose remaining entries are
      arbitrary elements of $Delta$.],
    [There are precisely $r$ maximal orders containing $Lambda$, and $Lambda$ is
      their intersection.],
  )
] <th:local-hereditary-orders-block-form>

To conclude this section, we record without proof the analogue of
@th:janusz-maximal-order-tensor-product:

#theorem(title: [Janusz~@bib:Janusz1979])[
  Let $R$ be a complete d.v.r. with finite residue field and quotient field $K$,
  and let $Lambda_1$ and $Lambda_2$ be $R$-orders in f.d. simple separable
  $K$-algebras. Then $Lambda_1 times.o_R Lambda_2$ is a hereditary $R$-order if
  and only if both $Lambda_1$ and $Lambda_2$ are hereditary, and one of them is
  a full matrix ring over a d.v.r. which is unramified over $R$.
] <th:janusz-hereditary-order-tensor-product>
#idx("Janusz's Theorem", target: [@th:janusz-hereditary-order-tensor-product])

As a special case, we obtain

#corollary[
  Hereditary orders remain hereditary under unramified extensions of the ground
  ring.
] <cor:unramified-hereditary-order-scalar-extension>

#source(600)
#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in the $K$-algebra $A$, and let $T$ be the
  ordinary trace map from $A$ to $K$. Suppose that
  $Lambda=R x_1 plus.o dots plus.o R x_m$, and that
  $ alpha=det[T(x_i x_j)]_(1<=i,j<=m) $
  is a nonzero element of $R$. Show that the localization $Lambda_P$ is a
  maximal $R_P$-order in $A$ for every maximal ideal $P$ of $R$ such that
  $alpha in.not P$.

  #hint([@exc:order-trace-unit-local-maximality])[
    Let $Lambda'$ be an $R$-order in $A$ containing $Lambda$. As in the proof of
    @eq:integral-subring-trace-bound, we have
    $Lambda' subset.eq alpha^(-1)Lambda$. Therefore $Lambda'_P=Lambda_P$
    whenever $alpha in.not P$.
  ]
] <exc:order-trace-unit-local-maximality>

#exercise[
  Let $G$ be a finite group, and suppose $|G|$ is a unit in $R$. Deduce from
  Exercise~@exc:order-trace-unit-local-maximality that $R G$ is a maximal
  $R$-order in $K G$.
] <exc:invertible-group-order-maximal-group-ring>

#exercise[
  Prove that a non-maximal order cannot be a principal ideal ring.

  #hint([@exc:nonmaximal-order-not-principal-ideal-ring])[
    Let $Lambda subset Lambda'$ be $R$-orders, and choose a nonzero $alpha in R$
    such that $alpha Lambda' subset.eq Lambda$, so $alpha Lambda'$ is a left
    ideal of $Lambda$. If $alpha Lambda'=Lambda x$ for some $x in A$, then
    $Lambda'=Lambda y$ for some $y$. Hence $Lambda'=Lambda' y$, so
    $y in u(Lambda')$. Therefore
    $Lambda'=Lambda' y^(-1)=(Lambda y)y^(-1)=Lambda$, a contradiction.
  ]
] <exc:nonmaximal-order-not-principal-ideal-ring>

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in a $K$-algebra $A$, and let $J$ be a two-sided
  ideal in $Lambda$ such that $K dot J=A$. Show that $J$ is a prime ideal if and
  only if $J$ is a maximal two-sided ideal.

  #hint([@exc:prime-maximal-order-ideals])[
    Let $J$ be maximal, and let $S dot T subset.eq J$ where $S$, $T$ are
    two-sided ideals. If $S subset.eq.not J$ and $T subset.eq.not J$, then
    $S+J=T+J=Lambda$. But then
    $ J supset.eq (S+J)(T+J)=Lambda, $
    a contradiction.

    Conversely, let $J$ be prime, and set $P=J inter R$, a prime ideal of $R$.
    Note that $P!=0$ since $K dot J=A$. Let $overline(Lambda)=Lambda slash J$,
    $overline(R)=R slash P$; then $overline(Lambda)$ is a f.d.
    $overline(R)$-algebra. If $"rad" overline(Lambda)!=0$, its inverse image in
    $Lambda$ is a two-sided ideal $S$ containing $J$ such that $S^k subset.eq J$
    for some $k$, whence $S subset.eq J$, which is impossible. Thus
    $"rad" overline(Lambda)=0$, so $overline(Lambda)$ is semisimple. If
    $overline(Lambda)$ is not simple, then there exist two-sided ideals
    $overline(S)$, $overline(T)$ in $overline(Lambda)$ such that
    $overline(S) dot overline(T)=0$, $overline(S)!=0$, $overline(T)!=0$; then
    $S dot T subset.eq J$, a contradiction. Thus $overline(Lambda)$ is a simple
    artinian ring, and has no nontrivial two-sided ideals. Thus $J$ is maximal.
  ]
] <exc:prime-maximal-order-ideals>

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in a central simple $K$-algebra. Show that
  there is a bijection between the set of maximal ideals $P$ of $R$ and the set
  of prime ideals $frak(p)$ of $Lambda$, given by
  $ P=R inter frak(p), quad frak(p)=Lambda inter "rad" Lambda_P. $
  Further, if $P arrow.l.r frak(p)$, then $P Lambda=frak(p)^e$ for some $e$.

  #hint([@exc:maximal-order-primes-over-base])[
    Given any prime ideal $frak(p)$, we put $P=R inter frak(p)$,
    $overline(Lambda)=Lambda slash frak(p)$, $overline(R)=R slash P$. Then
    $overline(Lambda)$ is a simple artinian $overline(R)$-algebra, and $P$ is a
    maximal ideal of $R$. We have
    $
      overline(Lambda) tilde.equiv R_P times.o_R overline(Lambda) tilde.equiv
      Lambda_P slash frak(p)Lambda_P,
    $
    #source(601)
    since the elements of $R-P$ act invertibly on $overline(Lambda)$. Since
    $overline(Lambda)$ is simple, we have
    $frak(p)Lambda_P supset.eq "rad" Lambda_P$. To prove equality, suppose that
    $ P Lambda_P=product_(i=1)^g frak(p)_i^(e_i), $
    where $g>1$. Choose sequences ${x_n}$, ${y_n}$ of elements of $Lambda$, such
    that
    $
      x_n equiv 1 "mod" frak(p)_1^n, quad x_n equiv 0 "mod" (frak(p)_2 dots
        frak(p)_g)^n,
    $
    $
      y_n equiv 1 "mod" frak(p)_g^n, quad y_n equiv 0 "mod" (frak(p)_1 dots
        frak(p)_(g-1))^n.
    $
    If $hat(A)_P$ denotes the $P$-adic completion of $A$, then $x_n->x$,
    $y_n->y$, with $x,y$ idempotents in $hat(A)_P$. Clearly $x y=0$, and
    furthermore $x$ and $y$ are in the center of $hat(A)_P$, since for all
    $a in Lambda$ and all $n$,
    $
      a x_n equiv x_n a, quad a y_n equiv y_n a "mod" (frak(p)_1 dots
        frak(p)_g)^n.
    $
    Passing to the limit, $x$ and $y$ commute with $Lambda$, hence with
    $A=K Lambda$ and its completion. This is impossible, since $hat(A)_P$ is a
    central simple $hat(K)_P$-algebra. Therefore $g=1$ so
    $P Lambda_P=(frak(p)Lambda_P)^e$ for some $e$. It follows that
    $frak(p)Lambda_P="rad" Lambda_P$.

    For any maximal ideal $Q$ of $R$ with $Q!=P$, we have
    $R_Q times.o_R overline(Lambda)=0$, whence $Lambda_Q=frak(p)_Q$. But then
    $
      frak(p)=frak(p)_P inter (op("⋂", limits: #true)_Q frak(p)_Q)=frak(p)_P
      inter Lambda="rad"
      Lambda_P inter Lambda.
    $
    Conversely, given $P$ we choose any prime ideal $frak(p)$ of $Lambda$ with
    $P Lambda subset.eq frak(p)$. Then $P=R inter frak(p)$, so by the above
    discussion $frak(p)=Lambda inter "rad" Lambda_P$. This proves the existence
    of a bijection $P arrow.l.r frak(p)$. Finally, if $frak(q)$ is any prime
    ideal of $Lambda$ occurring in the factorization of $P Lambda$, then (as
    above) $frak(q)=Lambda inter "rad" Lambda_P$, whence $frak(q)=frak(p)$.
  ]
] <exc:maximal-order-primes-over-base>

#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a full left $Lambda$-lattice in $A$, where $Lambda$ is a maximal
  $R$-order in the $K$-algebra $A$. Show that $O_r lr((M))$ is also a maximal
  $R$-order in $A$, and that
  $ M^(-1)M=O_r lr((M)), quad M M^(-1)=Lambda. $
  Prove also that $(M^(-1))^(-1)=M$.

  #hint([@exc:maximal-lattice-inverse-right-order])[
    Put $Gamma=O_r lr((M)) tilde.equiv "End"_Lambda M$ as $R$-modules. Since $M$
    is left $Lambda$-projective by @th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary, it follows
    that
    $ Hom_Lambda lr((M,Lambda)) times.o_Lambda M->Gamma $
    is a surjective $R$-linear map. But
    $
      Hom_Lambda lr((M,Lambda)) tilde.equiv {x in A:M x subset.eq
        Lambda}=M^(-1),
    $
    which implies that $M^(-1)M=Gamma$.

    On the other hand, $M M^(-1)=Lambda$ by
    @eq:maximal-left-order-inverse-product. If $Gamma subset.eq Gamma'$, where
    $Gamma'$ is an $R$-order in $A$, then
    $M Gamma' M^(-1) supset.eq M M^(-1)=Lambda$, so $M Gamma' M^(-1)$ is an
    $R$-order containing $Lambda$. Therefore $M Gamma' M^(-1)=Lambda$, whence
    $Gamma'=M^(-1)Lambda M=Gamma$. Thus $Gamma$ is maximal. Finally,
    $M(M^(-1))^(-1)=Lambda$ implies that $(M^(-1))^(-1)=M$.
  ]
] <exc:maximal-lattice-inverse-right-order>

#source(602)
#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a left $Lambda$-lattice, where $Lambda$ is a maximal $R$-order in
  the $K$-algebra $A$. Show that $"End"_Lambda M$ is a maximal $R$-order in
  $"End"_A K M$.

  #hint([@exc:maximal-lattice-endomorphism-order])[
    First pass to $P$-adic completions and to the simple components. After
    conjugation, write $Lambda=M_r lr((Delta))$ in the notation of
    @th:local-matrix-maximal-orders. Put $P=Delta^r$, with its natural left
    $Lambda$-action. Morita equivalence gives $M tilde.equiv P^((k))$ for some
    $k$, and $"End"_Lambda M tilde.equiv M_k lr((Delta^circle.small))$, a
    maximal order in $M_k lr((D^circle.small))$.
  ]
] <exc:maximal-lattice-endomorphism-order>

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in a f.d. semisimple $K$-algebra $A$. Show that
  $Lambda$ is left hereditary if and only if $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((I,J))=0$ for
  each pair of left ideals $I$, $J$ of $Lambda$.

  #hint([@exc:hereditary-order-ideal-ext-criterion])[
    Assume $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((I,J))=0$ for all $I$ and $J$. By
    Exercise~@exc:lattice-embedding-finite-free-order-module, every left
    $Lambda$-lattice $L$ can be embedded in a free $Lambda$-lattice
    $Lambda^((k))$ for some $k$. We now use induction on $k$ to show that
    $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((I,L))=0$ for all $I$. For $k=1$, the result follows from
    the hypothesis. Now let $k>1$; as in the proof of
    @prop:hereditary-free-submodules, there exists a $Lambda$-exact sequence
    $ 0->L_0->L->I_0->0, $
    with $I_0$ an ideal of $Lambda$ and $L_0$ a sublattice of $Lambda^((k-1))$.
    Then the result at $k-1$ implies it at $k$, and so
    $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((I,L))=0$ for every $Lambda$-lattice $L$ and for all left
    ideals $I$ of $Lambda$. Given $I$, there exists a $Lambda$-exact sequence
    $ 0->L->Lambda^((r))->I->0 $
    for some $r$. From $"Ext"_Lambda^1 lr((I,L))=0$ we obtain
    $Lambda^((r)) tilde.equiv L plus.o I$, so $I$ is $Lambda$-projective.
  ]
] <exc:hereditary-order-ideal-ext-criterion>

#exercise[
  Given $K="algebraic number field"$, $R="alg. int." {K}$,
  $G=chevron.l x:x^n=1 chevron.r$ a cyclic group, $S=R[omega_n]$, where
  $omega_n$ is a primitive $n$-th root of~1. Show that if
  $S!="alg. int." {K(omega_n)}$, then there exist ideals $I$, $J$ of $S$ such
  that $"Ext"_(R G)^1 lr((I,J))!=0$. Here, $x$ acts on $S$ and on its ideals as
  multiplication by $omega_n$.

  #hint([@exc:nonintegrally-closed-cyclotomic-order-extensions])[
    (Gudivok~@bib:Gudivok1967). The proof of
    @th:diederichsen-cyclotomic-extensions (Step~2) shows that
    $"Ext"_(R G)^1 lr((I,J))="Ext"_S^1 lr((I,J))$. If
    $S!="alg. int." {K(omega_n)}$, then $S$ is not hereditary, since
    $
      S "hereditary"=>S="Dedekind domain"
      \ =>S "integrally closed in" K(omega_n)
      =>S="alg. int." {K(omega_n)}.
    $
    Now use Exercise~@exc:hereditary-order-ideal-ext-criterion. An analogous
    result holds when $K$ is a $P$-adic field. Further, there are examples where
    $R[omega_n]!="alg. int." {K(omega_n)}$. Find one!
  ]
] <exc:nonintegrally-closed-cyclotomic-order-extensions>

#exercise[
  Prove directly the following special case of
  @th:global-maximal-orders-endomorphism-lattices: Let $A="End"_K V$ where $V$
  is an $n$-dimensional $K$-space. Then every maximal $R$-order in $A$ is of the
  form $"End"_R lr((M))$ for some full $R$-lattice $M$ in $V$, and every such
  order is maximal. In particular, if $R$ is a P.I.D., then every maximal
  $R$-order in $M_n lr((K))$ is conjugate to $M_n lr((R))$.

  #hint([@exc:matrix-maximal-order-endomorphisms])[
    Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in $A$, and let $M$ be a full
    $Lambda$-lattice in $V$. Then $Lambda subset.eq "End"_R lr((M))=$ $R$-order
    in $A$, whence $Lambda="End"_R lr((M))$. On the other hand, for any full
    $R$-lattice $M$ in $V$, we show that $"End"_R lr((M))$ is maximal. By
    @th:maximal-orders-localization, it suffices to treat the case where $R$ is
    local; assume thus that $R$ is a P.I.D. For any $R$-order $Gamma$ containing
    $M_n lr((R))$, let $bold(X)=(alpha_(i j)) in Gamma$, with each
    $alpha_(i j) in K$. For fixed $i$, $j$, we have
    $e_(1 i) bold(X)e_(j 1)="diag"(alpha_(i j),0,dots,0) in Gamma$. This
    diagonal matrix is integral over $R$, so $alpha_(i j) in R$. Thus
    $Gamma=M_n lr((R))$.
  ]
] <exc:matrix-maximal-order-endomorphisms>

#source(603)
#exercise[
  Let $R$ be a d.v.r. with quotient field $K$, and let $Lambda$ be a maximal
  $R$-order in a separable $K$-algebra $A$. Let $M$ and $N$ be left
  $Lambda$-lattices such that $K M tilde.equiv K N$ as left $A$-modules. Show
  that $M tilde.equiv N$ as $Lambda$-modules.

  #hint([@exc:dvr-maximal-order-lattice-isomorphism])[
    Let $P$ be the maximal ideal of $R$, and let $hat(M)$ denote the $P$-adic
    completion of $M$. It suffices to prove that $hat(M) tilde.equiv hat(N)$
    (see @prop:module-isomorphism-completion). Changing notation, let $R$ be a
    complete d.v.r. By @th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components, $Lambda$
    decomposes into a direct sum of maximal orders in the Wedderburn components
    of $A$. Each $Lambda$-lattice has a corresponding decomposition, so it
    suffices to prove the result for the case where $A$ is a simple algebra
    whose center $F$ is a finite separable extension of $K$. Let $S$ be the
    integral closure of $R$ in $F$, so $Lambda$ is a maximal $S$-order in the
    central simple $F$-algebra $A$ (by
    @th:maximal-orders-wedderburn-components).

    Let $M$ be any left $Lambda$-lattice, so $M$ is $Lambda$-projective by
    @th:maximal-orders-are-hereditary, and therefore
    $M=op("⨁", limits: #true) M_i$ where the ${M_i}$ are indecomposable
    projective $Lambda$-lattices. Let $J="rad" Lambda$; by
    @cor:indecomposable-projective-top, each $M_i slash J M_i$ is a simple left
    $(Lambda slash J)$-module. But $Lambda slash J$ is a simple artinian ring,
    by Exercises~@exc:prime-maximal-order-ideals and
    @exc:maximal-order-primes-over-base. Thus the ${M_i slash J M_i}$ are
    mutually isomorphic, whence so are the ${M_i}$. It follows at once that the
    isomorphism class of $M$ is completely determined by the $R$-rank of $M$.
  ]
] <exc:dvr-maximal-order-lattice-isomorphism>

#exercise[
  Let $A$ be a commutative separable $K$-algebra, and let $Lambda$ be its
  maximal $R$-order. Let $L$ be a finite separable unramified extension of $K$,
  and let $S$ be the integral closure of $R$ in $L$. Show that
  $S times.o_R Lambda$ is a maximal $S$-order in $L times.o_K A$. This is a
  special case of @cor:unramified-maximal-order-scalar-extension.

